1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,707
JAMIE: Eli Lilly's up five point five percent while healthcare stocks lap AI hype, but somehow Red Lobster's Endless Shrimp buffet just bankrupted the company—which means the real bubble was never in semiconductors.

2
00:00:10,707 --> 00:00:38,611
ALEX: So yesterday we watched Eli Lilly jump five point five percent on the back of healthcare beating AI. Well, that thesis held overnight. The market's not hedging anymore; it's just picking a lane. And here's the thing: Red Lobster's shrimp buffet didn't bankrupt them because they were too ambitious about seafood logistics. It bankrupted them because unit economics don't care about nostalgia. Same math that's killing them is what's going to cull the AI startups burning cash on inference costs they can't pass to customers.

3
00:00:38,611 --> 00:01:16,574
ALEX: S&P up zero point zero six percent, Nasdaq down zero point one one percent, V I X climbed two point zero six to nineteen point twenty-eight. So we're basically flat on the broad market while volatility's creeping higher. The real story: Eli Lilly's up five point five percent again, Netflix up five point zero one, Salesforce up four point one three—healthcare and streaming and enterprise software are having their moment. Meanwhile Cisco's down two point six seven, Broadcom down two point six three, A M D down two point zero eight. The semis are getting liquidated while the market's still deciding whether healthcare actually cures inflation or just cures the fear of missing out on healthcare.

4
00:01:16,574 --> 00:01:27,930
JAMIE: So the market's not confused about what it wants—it's just rotating out of the thing that was supposed to save us six weeks ago. Is that the breadth problem, or is that just Tuesday?

5
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:44,478
ALEX: That's the breadth problem wearing a Tuesday costume. S&P's barely moving because you've got this narrow band of mega-cap winners propping up the whole index while the Russell two thousand's flat and the Nasdaq's underwater. The market's not broadening out—it's concentrating. That's a warning sign that looks like a rally.

6
00:01:44,478 --> 00:02:01,350
MAYA: Okay but real question: is healthcare actually solving something or are we just in the phase where if you're not a semiconductor company you're suddenly investable again? Because that feels like the market's just flipping the channel when the last show got boring, not like we found something that actually works.

7
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:16,275
ALEX: Healthcare's solving something—drug efficacy, aging populations, actual revenue. But you're right that the rotation is also just rotation. Money's moving from inflation hedges into defensive names and growth that's not growth—it's just 'not semiconductors.' That's not a thesis. That's musical chairs with better P.E. ratios.

8
00:02:16,275 --> 00:02:23,414
JAMIE: So we're not buying healthcare because we think people get sick more. We're buying it because we're scared of everything else.

9
00:02:23,414 --> 00:02:38,663
ALEX: Red Lobster's customers don't care about volume—they care about the all-you-can-eat price. So you're left selling at a fixed price point while your cost per pound climbs. It's like agreeing to sell your house for the same price every year while inflation runs at eight percent.

10
00:02:38,663 --> 00:02:50,020
MAYA: Okay but here's the thing that kills me—Red Lobster could have just... not run the Endless Shrimp promotion every single week. Like, why is a restaurant chain letting a supplier write their menu strategy?

11
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:58,456
ALEX: Because Red Lobster was already underwater. When you're drowning, you don't negotiate with the guy throwing you a rope—you just grab it and hope it holds.

12
00:02:58,456 --> 00:03:09,812
JAMIE: So the real story is that a restaurant chain couldn't figure out how to make money, and then a supplier became the de facto operator of their P&L. That's not a lawsuit—that's an autopsy.

13
00:03:09,812 --> 00:03:27,009
MAYA: And now everyone's pointing fingers at the shrimp. The shrimp was never the problem—the problem is that unlimited food at a fixed price point can only work if your cost structure goes to zero, which it doesn't, which is why Red Lobster is gone and we're all reading court filings about crustaceans.

14
00:03:27,009 --> 00:03:34,147
ALEX: Citi just upgraded Allegiant on merger synergies while the broader airline sector's posting earnings beats. Consolidation's not a narrative anymore—it's math.

15
00:03:34,147 --> 00:03:41,934
JAMIE: So we're not betting on travel demand picking up. We're betting on two struggling carriers merging and suddenly discovering efficiency nobody else found.

16
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:58,158
ALEX: The earnings beat is real—load factors are up, pricing's holding. But synergies are the fantasy. You're combining two airlines that both have thin margins, old fleets, and labor agreements they can't touch. The merger doesn't create revenue; it just promises to cut costs that are already cut to the bone.

17
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:07,243
MAYA: Trump's easing pressure on Fed Chair Kevin Warsh while inflation's still at four percent—core inflation's sticky, rates aren't moving, and suddenly everyone's pretending the problem solved itself.

18
00:04:07,243 --> 00:04:14,705
JAMIE: So the Fed Chair gets a reprieve the moment the administration decides it wants one, regardless of whether prices actually stopped climbing.

19
00:04:14,705 --> 00:04:30,280
ALEX: Warsh hasn't cut rates yet. Trump's advisers are just signaling they'll be patient if he does. But inflation's not patient—it's still running above target. The political theater here is that everyone's acting like we've solved inflation when all we've done is decide to stop talking about it.

20
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:40,987
MAYA: Meta thinks gambling is the future of engagement, so they're rolling it out across their platforms because nothing says 'we've run out of ideas' like monetizing the same scroll with slot machines.

21
00:04:40,987 --> 00:04:47,801
JAMIE: Is this a revenue play or a 'we measured engagement and it went up so we're calling it strategy' play?

22
00:04:47,801 --> 00:05:05,647
MAYA: It's both. Meta clones whatever works on TikTok, measures the time-on-app spike, and calls it innovation. Gambling metrics are insane because people can't stop. But the moment regulators notice you're running a casino inside a social network, that metric becomes a liability. They're chasing engagement without asking if the engagement they're chasing is legal.

23
00:05:05,647 --> 00:05:12,785
JAMIE: Imposter scams topped the FTC's fraud report for the fifth straight year—three point five billion dollars stolen, same con, year after year.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/26/imposter-scams-led-fraud-reports-to-ftc-in-2025-3point5-billion-losses.html

24
00:05:12,785 --> 00:05:24,790
ALEX: Five years of the same attack vector and we're not getting better at defending it. Someone calls, says they're the IRS or the bank, and people hand over money because the psychological pressure is faster than skepticism.

25
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:39,716
MAYA: The infrastructure to prevent this exists—call authentication, real-time verification, S M S alerts. But the victim has to opt in, and the scammer just has to dial. We've decided it's easier to let people lose three point five billion than to rebuild how phones work.

26
00:05:39,716 --> 00:05:57,886
MAYA: Cardiac arrests are spiking during heat waves, and here's the part that's landing weird with cardiologists: it's not just the elderly and frail anymore. Young, fit people are dropping during runs in extreme heat. Experts are now telling people to avoid running in extreme heat and avoid drinking too many beers at the same time.

27
00:05:57,886 --> 00:06:07,944
JAMIE: So the research basically found a guy named Derek who thought the solution to heat stroke was a cold I P A, and now we have a public health advisory.

28
00:06:07,944 --> 00:06:29,035
ALEX: The mechanism is straightforward: heat stress plus dehydration plus alcohol vasodilation equals arrhythmia. Your core temperature climbs, your electrolytes shift, and alcohol makes your blood vessels dump heat faster than your body can compensate. The warning isn't about Derek—it's that the combination is lethal in ways people don't intuitively understand. You feel fine at mile two. You're not fine at mile two point five.

29
00:06:29,035 --> 00:06:51,747
MAYA: Right, but the fact that we needed a study to tell people 'intensive exercise plus alcohol in a heat dome is bad' tells you something about how much people trust their own judgment. The medical literature is basically saying: your body is not a self-correcting system. You can be young, run marathons, and still end up in cardiac arrest because you thought a post-run beer was a recovery strategy.

30
00:06:51,747 --> 00:06:58,236
MAYA: Red Lobster proved that when your supplier becomes your board of directors, bankruptcy is just a slow-motion hostage negotiation.

31
00:06:58,236 --> 00:07:02,455
ALEX: And the shrimp wins either way—gets eaten or gets deposed in court.

32
00:07:02,455 --> 00:07:06,997
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
